## Settled case
**M-2024-0018 Whitfield Family Trust v. Continental Securities** — settled 2024-12-15, gross $1,800,000, Sterling fee $594,000 (33%), cost reimbursement $45,000, net to client $1,161,000. The other two contingency matters (Meadowbrook, Ferndale) are active and out of scope.

## Posting status (per the assignment rule)
- **Original settlement receipt — already posted** in the QBO JE register as JE-OB-000842 (fee $594,000 → 1000/4200) and JE-OB-000843 (cost reimbursement $45,000 → 1000/1300). **No journal entry proposed** for these, nor for the already-posted AP bills/payments.
- **Post-settlement holdback/clawback — not posted.** This is the only item requiring a proposal.

## The holdback (from the Jan 14, 2025 memo)
A competing investor claim filed in **late Nov 2024** (before year-end) caused the administrator to recall **$90,000** of gross proceeds to escrow. Sterling's fee is recalculated on the adjusted gross:
- Adjusted gross = $1,800,000 − $90,000 = $1,710,000
- Recalculated fee = 33% × $1,710,000 = **$564,300**
- **Sterling excess-fee return obligation = $594,000 − $564,300 = $29,700** (the other $60,300 is the client's reduced-net share, off Sterling's books; the $45,000 cost reimbursement is not subject to recall).

## Proposed journal entry (the only one)
Type I subsequent event (condition existed at 12/31/2024), booked 2024-12-15:
- Dr 4200 Contingency Fee Revenue **$29,700**
- Cr 2XXX Settlement Clawback Payable — Due to Settlement Administrator **$29,700**

Section 7.4 interest (~$122 by the memo date) is a **2025** Type II event (grace period was still open at 12/31/2024) — disclosed, not accrued in 2024.

## Tax-loss treatment
The $29,700 is **not** a theft/casualty/bad-debt/capital loss — it's a civil clawback, conceded by outside counsel. Supported treatment: **revenue adjustment** flowing through Form 1065. The $90,000 escrow has a reasonable prospect of recovery (Q1 2025) → review/disclosure only. No Form 4684. The 2024-vs-2025 (§1341) tax timing is reserved for CPA/tax counsel.

